The parts of the mammalian eye are (each may be given with its function):

1. **Sclera (sclerotic coat)**: tough white outer layer that protects the eye and keeps its shape;
2. **Cornea**: transparent front part of the sclera which lets light in and refracts it;
3. **Conjunctiva**: thin transparent membrane covering the front of the eye;
4. **Choroid**: dark layer containing blood vessels and pigment that prevents internal reflection;
5. **Iris**: coloured muscular ring that controls the size of the pupil;
6. **Pupil**: opening in the centre of the iris through which light enters;
7. **Lens**: transparent, elastic, biconvex structure that focuses light on the retina;
8. **Ciliary body (ciliary muscles)** and **suspensory ligaments**: hold the lens and change its shape;
9. **Aqueous humour**: watery fluid in front of the lens that maintains the shape of the front of the eye and refracts light;
10. **Vitreous humour**: jelly-like fluid behind the lens that keeps the shape of the eyeball;
11. **Retina**: inner layer containing the light-sensitive **rods** and **cones**; the **yellow spot (fovea)** is the region of sharpest vision;
12. **Optic nerve**: carries impulses from the retina to the brain; the point where it leaves the eye is the **blind spot**.
